  "strategy": "We are to minimize the objective function for a step function with positive or zero coefficients: score = (2 * n * max_convolve_value) / (sum_a)^2, where max_convolve_value is the maximum of the linear convolution of the sequence (mode='full').\n\nThe previous best sequence achieved a score of 1.505006. We are to find a sequence with a score lower than 1.5027.\n\nOur approach will be to use a genetic algorithm (GA) with elitism and simulated binary crossover (SBX) along with polynomial mutation. We'll explore several sequence lengths (512, 768, 1024) and use the scored from the evaluator to guide the search.\n\nKey steps:\n1. Asymmetric Initialization: We'll start with an asymmetric sequence for each chosen length.\n2. Genetic Algorithm:\n   - Use a population of sequences (convert each sequence to a list).\n   - Apply crossover and mutation to evolve the candidate.\n   - Use the Frank-Wolfe direction update based on the constraints from the convolution.\n\nWe'll calculate the initial population using a combination of exponential and sine decays for asymmetry. Then, we'll use a fitness function that is the negative of the score (since GA minimizes fitness). We'll use elitism to keep the best candidate from each generation.\n\nParameters for GA:\n- Population size: 50.\n- Mutation probability: 0.05.\n- Crossover probability: 0.8.\n- Generations: Until time runs out or the best score doesn't improve.\n- We'll time each generation and allow up to 900 seconds for the entire GA run.\n\nAfter finding the best candidate through the GA, we'll convert to a Python list and return it.",
